From: EglantinePS5 <noreply@eglantineps5.local>
Subject: [PATCH] Migrate 0323-rebase-04-descriptor-tables.patch

Source: 0323-rebase-04-descriptor-tables.patch
texture_cache: size descriptor tables to their limit

Port of PR #277 (nowhere02, Djihads80/Eglantine, open), commit in refs/pull/277. GPL-3.0-or-later,
as the core. Our tree also has bound_ids, which the PR does not know, so those grow with it.

Every descriptor table took at least 0x80001 entries: 16 MiB, plus 2 MiB of ids. Up to four tables
per GPU channel (graphics and compute, images and samplers), and run60's log shows channels 2, 3 and
5 at least. Now a table holds the next power of two above its limit and never shrinks. Changed from
the PR: never more than the old rounding gave. Plain bit_ceil is larger for some limits (0x100000
needed 72 MiB against 54), and this build runs out of memory. The most a table saves is 18 MiB, at
a small limit; at 0x7ffff and up it saves nothing.

Every reader stops at current_limit first (GetSamplerId, VisitImageView, DEBUG_ASSERT in Read), so
entries past the limit are never touched. Switch, added for next18: EDEN_NX2_TIGHT_DESCRIPTOR_TABLES=1,
default off. Unset, the sizes and the resizing are as before.

Proven: the arithmetic, and that next14's DescriptorTable::Refresh adds 0x80001 (0x15af2f8).
Not proven: how much this gives back. The EDEN_HEAP_SITE lines of run57 and run61 show no 16 MiB
vector from Refresh, and that is not explained. If a game's limit is large the saving is smaller.

Core patch. Nothing here has run on a console.

diff --git a/src/video_core/texture_cache/descriptor_table.h b/src/video_core/texture_cache/descriptor_table.h
index c34083cf20..d9e65c85e0 100644
--- a/src/video_core/texture_cache/descriptor_table.h
+++ b/src/video_core/texture_cache/descriptor_table.h
@@ -7,6 +7,8 @@
 #pragma once
 
 #include <algorithm>
+#include <bit>
+#include <cstdlib>
 #include <cstring>
 #include <vector>
 
@@ -70,15 +72,25 @@ public:
     void Refresh(GPUVAddr gpu_addr, u32 limit) noexcept {
         current_gpu_addr = gpu_addr;
         current_limit = limit;
-        // Mario Brothership reallocates a lot of times, so use aggressive pre-alloc sizes
-        // std::vector<T> by default uses quadratic growth, but that isn't even enough to satisfy brothership
-        const size_t num_descriptors = ((limit + 0x80000) & (~0x7ffff)) + 1;
-        read_descriptors.resize(Common::DivCeil(num_descriptors, 64U));
+        // EDEN_NX2_TIGHT_DESCRIPTOR_TABLES=1: round up geometrically. Growth stays amortized without
+        // 16 MiB for every small table (0x80001 entries of 32 bytes, plus 2 MiB of ids, is the
+        // minimum otherwise). Never past the old rounding to 0x80000, which is the smaller one for
+        // some large limits, and such a table never shrinks.
+        static const bool tight = [] {
+            const char* const value = std::getenv("EDEN_NX2_TIGHT_DESCRIPTOR_TABLES");
+            return value != nullptr && value[0] == '1';
+        }();
+        const size_t old_rounding = ((limit + 0x80000) & (~0x7ffff)) + 1;
+        const size_t num_descriptors =
+            tight ? (std::min)(std::bit_ceil(static_cast<size_t>(limit) + 1), old_rounding)
+                  : old_rounding;
+        if (!tight || descriptors.size() < num_descriptors) {
+            read_descriptors.resize(Common::DivCeil(num_descriptors, 64U));
+            descriptors.resize(num_descriptors);
+            bound_ids.resize(num_descriptors, Id{0});
+        }
         ClearReadDescriptors();
         invalidated = false;
-        //
-        descriptors.resize(num_descriptors);
-        bound_ids.resize(num_descriptors, Id{0});
     }
 
     std::vector<u64> read_descriptors;
